{
  "image_name": "990aa9f993013bd5f9022a435f6b1bdd.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several ways nucleic-acid sensors can be built so that target binding changes fluorescence. Across all designs, a fluorophore and quencher are arranged so the optical signal depends on whether the analyte induces a structural rearrangement.\n\nThe top left compares two “bimodal” strategies. In one, target binding folds the sequence so fluorophore and quencher come together, turning the signal off. In the other, binding displaces one labeled strand from another, separating the labels and turning the signal on. The top right shows a split-aptamer approach: two fragments assemble around the analyte. Depending on where the labels are placed, this assembly either brings fluorophore and quencher together for signal loss or keeps them apart for signal gain.\n\nThe lower left illustrates structure-switching SELEX, where aptamers are selected on a solid support for their ability to change conformation and release from a complementary capture strand upon recognizing the target. The lower right then shows how such selected sequences are used as structure-switching sensors: target binding reshapes the aptamer, disrupts base pairing, and separates the quencher from the fluorophore, producing a fluorescence increase. Overall, the figure emphasizes target-induced folding or assembly as the basis of aptamer sensor readout.",
